Bugs:
1. ✅ The self-lifting floor — spawned objects soared into the sky at 30 m/s and every later spawn was teleported up to them. Reported against the shape generator law; it was not the law, it was physics. Done and verified (2026-08-14): diagnosed with `scratch/probes/coincident_spawn_probe.cpp`, fixed, and guarded by the new `tests/ground_plane_test.cpp` (6 checks; verified to FAIL all 6 with the fix reverted, producing exactly the reported numbers — highest y 46.60 after four clicks, still climbing to 136.60 three seconds later). Three faults, all one mistake — a subsystem deciding by list index what a being IS:
   (a) `World::update` fell back to `groundIdx = 1` when nothing carried `baseline=ground`, and a Zone's world starts EMPTY (EngineInit's baseline cube+ground go to the Ourverse's list, not the Zone's), so the SECOND being a Person spawned became the floor without mark or consent. Now: a tagged ground, or the y=0 plane. Same index assumption fixed in `Ourverse::onUpdate`.
   (b) `Physics::integrate` clamps the point it is handed; `updateBodies` handed it the object's transform ORIGIN while groundY names the floor's TOP surface. Every object buried its centre in the floor, and the being defining groundY sat necessarily half its own height below its own top — so the clamp lifted it half a height per substep, raising groundY, lifting it again, unbounded. Now: objects rest their BOTTOM on groundY (via `supportOffset`, read off the collision zone so rotation and scale are respected), and the ground is kinematic — not integrated at all. Note exempting the ground from the clamp alone is NOT enough: it then falls forever and drags groundY down with it (observed, then fixed).
   (c) The collision loop skipped `i == 1` as "the ground" on the same index assumption, so in a Zone's world the second being a Person spawned passed through everything. Fault (a) hid this one. Now skipped by `baseline` attribute, matching what `enforceCollisions` already did.
   NOT addressed (separate, still open): in "CursorSnap" placement `Tool::ShapeGenerator3D` leaves `cursorHitPos` at its previous value when the pick ray misses, so a click into empty space spawns at the last hit point rather than refusing. Harmless now that nothing soars, but it is the same "guess rather than refuse" shape that `ActionNode::Spawn` already fixed on the placement path.

